<<~ ahu #/invitation>>
! Example Zero Folio — Olympos, Tempes, Phlegra

This Folio presents one complete set of **expanded art sigils** for an artistic problem: which mountain passages survive, and which drawings warrant trust? The contiguous `folio-sigils` block carries the usable art direction, while the surrounding ahus preserve visual reasoning, methods, reference provenance and fictional boundaries. The companion Inscription offers the compact expression.

**Read:** one luminous region-scale plan-oblique panorama with green–ochre–snow hypsometric relief dominates; a vellum revision exposes an impossible painterly crossing; dark archaeological alternatives refuse one identity; a small green refuge challenges blanket desolation.

**Outside the image:** viewers, reviewers, artist biographies, source photos and references. **Inside:** the physical pinboard, its distinct paper media and any tiny figures within the subordinate drawings. No surrounding studio furniture or display apparatus.

**TW5 expression:** the contiguous Folio code block contains invocation calls only; declaration syntax and defaults live in the linked Procedures meme. The block carries pictorial studies, material treatments and consequential relations; external research URIs remain in the surrounding ahus, not in the transmitted art sigils.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/folio-sigils>>
!! Example Zero — complete Folio art sigils

Copy the **entire block below** as one expanded artistic invitation. The first `art()` call declares the overarching image; the subsequent studies, counterpoints and facture calls articulate subordinate pictorial and material questions. The copyable block contains artistic directions only. Source URIs and their provenance sit outside it in the `terrain-references` and `resources` research ahus, available alongside the Folio without turning into depicted labels, photos or credits.

**Procedure definitions (outside the copyable block):** `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/art-sigils/procedures` — [[Art Sigils Procedure Grammar|ha.ka.ba/lares/api/art-sigils/procedures]]. For native TW5 execution, load its `art()`, `art-study()`, `art-counterpoint()` and `art-facture()` declarations; an AI may interpret the calls directly without executing TW5. The companion compact [[Example Zero Inscription|ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/art-sigils/example-zero-inscription]] carries only one `art()` invocation.

```tiddlywiki
<<art worktype=`messy concept-art board` subject=`shattered Olympos, Rift of Tempes, Phlegra` read=`a beautiful high crossing fails a terrain test; relic identity and habitation remain contested` imagery=`regional plan-oblique landscape: green valleys, ochre ridges, snow heights, watersheds; vellum correction; embodied ledge; charcoal relic; green refuge; cyan, amber and violet scraps` treatment=`Tom Patterson and Bernhard Jenny shaded relief; Heinrich Berann panorama; Luka Rejec mythpunk travel; Piranesi/Ian Miller Phlegra charcoal; distinct papers` composition=`sunlit hypsometric watercolor map dominates; unequal pinned studies around it; broad airy sky and one dark study` relation=`red high route ends at cliff, white correction follows lower saddle; relic bone or machine?; refuge resists universal desolation` depiction-scope=`board fills frame; figures in inset studies`>>
<<art-study question=`Does the region connect?` form=`large oblique landform watercolor` proposal=`connected ridges, streams and saddles; green-to-ochre-to-snow altitude bands; shaded light and aerial haze` status=`painted terrain hypothesis`>>
<<art-facture scope=`upper panorama` support=`warm white cold-press paper` medium=`transparent watercolor and fine ink` handling=`granulating washes, quiet sky, readable landforms`>>
<<art-study question=`Where does the high route fail?` form=`blue-gray vellum over terrain sketch` proposal=`red path meets broken escarpment; white path traces lower saddle near the same drainage` status=`visible correction, provisional`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`painter's tempting high pass` second=`cartographer's lower saddle` tension=`scenery opposes foot travel` evidence=`repeated cliff, gully and ridge; red line breaks, white path bypasses`>>
<<art-facture scope=`route sheet` support=`thin translucent vellum` medium=`graphite, white and red pencil` handling=`erasures and competing path lines`>>
<<art-study question=`What confronts a hiker?` form=`small embodied ledge watercolor` proposal=`human figures, sheer drop and perilous descent contradict a distant easy-looking route` status=`ground-level witness`>>
<<art-study question=`What haunts Phlegra?` form=`heavy charcoal ruin and Bristol ink diagram` proposal=`stone, massive bone and machine parts compete without definitive identity` status=`unresolved`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`Piranesi/Ian Miller black relic impression` second=`thin-line engineering reconstruction` tension=`bone-like arcs clash with machinery` evidence=`shared silhouette, impossible joints and erased outlines`>>
<<art-facture scope=`Phlegra studies` support=`fibrous gray paper and smooth Bristol` medium=`rubbed charcoal, chalk and fine ink` handling=`dense uncertain ruin versus sparse technical conjecture`>>
<<art-study question=`What inhabits the scar?` form=`small spring-fed refuge gouache` proposal=`green terraces, repaired buildings and tiny people persist amid surrounding damage` status=`modest and unnamed`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`desolated Phlegra` second=`repaired green refuge` tension=`catastrophe meets care` evidence=`spring water, cultivation, shelters and small workers`>>
<<art-facture scope=`refuge and hazard scraps` support=`separate cream sheets` medium=`green gouache and cyan/amber/violet swatches` handling=`distinct luminous accents without fabricated hazard legend`>>
<<art-study question=`How does the region remain readable beyond one heroic summit?` form=`large plan-oblique hypsometric watercolor with shared watersheds` proposal=`Patterson and Jenny clarify ridges; Berann composes summit-to-valley continuity; Raisz selects distinctive landforms; Imhof balances tonal light and depth` status=`legible fictional terrain`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`the pale region-scale watercolor panorama` second=`the small bodily ledge and slope studies` tension=`elevated elegance conceals difficult footing` evidence=`the same cliff nose, stream cleft and saddle change scale; exposed travelers challenge the painter's confident passage`>>
<<art-study question=`Which marks keep ancient Phlegra ambiguous?` form=`dark charcoal corroded architecture beside measured-seeming ink reconstruction` proposal=`Piranesi scale and Ian Miller organic mechanical grime haunt the fragment; broken ribs suggest bone or machinery but joins resist either explanation` status=`unresolved testimony`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`luminous Olympos and unpainted white sky` second=`dense scar-blackened Phlegra charcoal` tension=`two local pictorial languages share one damaged region` evidence=`related ridgeline and debris align across unlike papers; light retains broad quiet, shadow concentrates uncertainty`>>
<<art-study question=`What do anomalous pigments communicate without false hazard science?` form=`separate cyan, amber and violet scratched pigment scraps` proposal=`localized Kilian Eng chromatic unease and Moebius open luminous air surround doubtful fragments` status=`visual differences without declared mechanism`>>
<<art-facture scope=`spaces between pinned papers` support=`warm board, pinholes, overlapping rough and translucent stocks` medium=`reserved negative space, shallow pin shadows, chalk traces` handling=`unpainted breathing room and irregular edges`>>
```

**Receiver scope:** The copyable Folio follows a practical 6,000-character ceiling, counting every character within its code block. It carries no URLs or procedure declarations. Research URI lists remain available in the surrounding `terrain-references` and `resources` ahus for source-aware receivers. Midjourney may still shorten or reinterpret a long structured prompt; the [[Example Zero Inscription|ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/art-sigils/example-zero-inscription]] offers a smaller alternative.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/visual-thesis>>
!! Dominant picture — regional terrain rather than a mountain portrait

The main painting offers a **cartographic thesis**: continuous plan-oblique fantasy relief across the shattered heights of Olympos, the Rift of Tempes and the scarred margins of Phlegra. A monumental summit attracts attention without swallowing the surrounding ridges, watersheds, passes, ravines, shelves and inhabited lower valleys. The viewer can mentally follow the region rather than simply admire a peak.

**Hypsometric tints** move from green lower lands through ochre exposed elevations to pale snow and rock. **Shaded relief** gives slopes their form; **atmospheric recession** gives distant terrain air; **plan-oblique composition** joins the terrain into an elevated spatial argument. None supplies surveyed elevations or a canon route. Preserve the broad luminous panorama and enough quiet sky for the landform to read.

The outer image shows the **flat pinned artboard alone**. No surrounding desk, room, wall perspective or physical terrain maquette. Embedded field studies may contain tiny travelers; neither the audience nor the artist enters the depicted studio by default. Supporting images surround and partly overlap the main painting without converting it into a grid of equal postcards.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/field-witnesses>>
!! Pinned witnesses — competing styles and narratives

* **Cartographer:** graphite on translucent blue-gray vellum disputes the painter's high crossing. The red route terminates at a recognizable cliff; the white correction follows a lower saddle. Both views share a cliff, drainage or landmark; erasures show revision without pretending to certify the path.
* **Embodied traveler:** a small eye-level watercolor or drybrush ledge study conveys exposure and passage at human scale without forcing the entire dominant panorama into a hiker's viewpoint.
* **Archaeologist:** a charcoal Phlegra impression on cool fibrous stock proposes colossal bone; a separate fine-ink Bristol reconstruction suggests machinery or stone. Preserve incompatible but plausible outlines, not one settled identity.
* **Inhabitant:** a small green gouache refuge with springs, terraces and repair work challenges blanket desolation. It should remain modest rather than a second glorious landscape.
* **Observer of anomalies:** cyan, amber and violet scraps explore different localized appearances; avoid fabricated mechanisms, measurements or authoritative legends.

These studies perform **distinct inquiries**, not decorative variety. Unequal finish, visible revisions and different material supports matter as much as a visually pleasing arrangement.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/questions>>
!! Four propositions in productive tension

**1 — Pass:** the relief panorama suggests a high crossing; a graphite vellum correction challenges it using the same cliff, lower saddle and drainage. Preserve both marks, erasures and unresolved survey uncertainty.

**2 — Remains:** the dark Phlegra studies read as giant bone, machine or stone depending on medium, contour and prior expectation. The competing outlines withhold any final identification.

**3 — Refuge:** an inhabited spring study complicates the claim that all Olympos terrain suffers permanent uninhabitability.

**4 — Hazards:** cyan, amber and violet scraps investigate distinct appearances. Their physical mechanisms remain unproven.

The papers communicate revision through actual erasures, changed routes and conflicting construction lines—not merely an inventory of captions saying the artists disagree.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/method>>
!! Support, marks, color and the limits of names

* **Relief:** Tom Patterson and Bernhard Jenny share the primary cartographic attractor: shaded relief and legible ridges; Heinrich Berann joins only in panoramic orchestration. Use source artists as **method-bearing references**, not literal name labels.
* **High light:** preserve broad pale upper sky, coherent plan-oblique terrain, relief hierarchy and a believable summit-to-valley descent. Watercolor cold-press behaves differently from depicted stone.
* **Route overlay:** vellum reads as pale translucent paper with graphite corrections, not another mountain cloud. The amended crossing challenges the painted route at a meaningful location.
* **Dark matter:** Piranesi informs ruined engineering; Ian Miller informs locally dense, corroded organic-mechanical studies. Keep them in Phlegra rather than turning the entire mountain into an impossible cathedral.
* **Small life:** quiet green gouache and repairs demonstrate use and inhabitation amid ongoing danger.
* **Color scraps:** localized cyan, amber and violet test pictorial alternatives. Avoid invented legends masquerading as measured hazards.
* **Yin reserve:** unpainted sky, uncertain relic identity, aborted lines, partially finished studies and quiet intervals. The richer Folio does not require a busier picture.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/assembly>>
!! Board assembly, pictorial argument, and what remains offstage

* **Composition:** a dominant region-scale plan-oblique hypsometric relief panorama across the upper work; supporting lower studies vary in size, orientation and resolution; charcoal relic study darkens one side while the modest refuge occupies a quieter counterweight. Avoid a uniformly polished portfolio grid.
* **Spatial representation:** shared ridges, passes, ravines, basins and drainage remain physiographically coherent; aerial painting, embedded route map and embodied ledge view can differ in spatial convention. A summit-to-lowland sea sweep suggests Berann and Jenny/Patterson, never a false surveyed metric legend.
* **Address and nested scope:** the maker, reference photographer and art director remain outside the image; tiny travelers occupy *inner* landscape studies only. Pinned pictures depict different interpretations, not detached fragments of one seamless scene.
* **Media behavior:** cold-press watercolor wash, translucent route vellum, rubbed charcoal on fibrous grey stock, sharp ink on smooth Bristol, small gouache and color scraps. Paper fiber, applied media, pictured bronze patina, geological strata and cloud recession obey different visual scales.
* **Value/light:** pale upper mountain atmosphere and quieter distant recession; locally corrosive black-charcoal and anomalous cyan/amber/violet near the wounds; avoid a global sepia wash or identical micro-grain over the entire board.
* **Inquiry:** two different route hypotheses, erased pass mark, alternative relic identification, some unfinished areas and unequal finish levels. Labels need not explain disagreement when marks already demonstrate it.
* **Withholding:** no settled divine identity, exact river survey, fabricated route distance, confirmed supernatural cause, automatic artist credit or floating distant city name. The blank sky performs compositional work.

**Audience/address:** a collaborator reviewing hypotheses about a setting. **Artifactual narrative:** a collection of studies accumulated across differing questions and opinions. **Subject narrative:** a sacred mountain badly wounded by ancient war, later smothered by maelstrom, now difficult but possible to traverse. Audience and hypothetical makers stay *outside* the image.

Primary first read: a sweeping high, luminous relief-aware Olympos panorama. Second read: a damaged passage and dark Phlegra/relic studies with substantially heavier line and contrast. On sustained looking: a route overlay visibly disputes the panorama, a charcoal alternative questions a giant-bone interpretation, and a modest refuge challenges the idea of universal uninhabitability.

Difference of opinion need not become explanatory text. Two mismatched drainage traces, an erased crossing, a stained scrap with revised colour, and a partly concealed earlier silhouette can communicate revision through their juxtaposition. Papers overlap because the artists compare something specific. Ground-level travel studies may inherit the embodied reference's viewpoint without depicting its photographer, while elevated paintings and map overlays draw on the aerial and terrain references for different tasks.

Do not mistake the physical support of the *outer board* for the depicted geological rock of an embedded study. Avoid equal-sized postcard panels, generalized antique parchment, or extravagant technical circles that displace terrain inquiry. The upper panorama can remain bright even when the Phlegra sheets darken.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/artist-register>>
!! Artist lineage by contribution

* **Tom Patterson** — shaded relief, mountain/seafloor coherence, colour and detail generalization.
* **Bernhard Jenny** — plan oblique, relief geometry, Swiss colour shading and aerial perspective.
* **Heinrich Berann** — panoramic landform orchestration from summit toward lowland and sea.
* **Eduard Imhof** — tonal hierarchy, illumination, calm depth in complexity.
* **Erwin Raisz** — memorable physiographic forms and purposeful terrain simplification.
* **Jean Giraud / Moebius** — luminous air, economical line, vast silent science-fantasy spaces.
* **Luka Rejec** — mythpunk travel, strange relics, gameable exploratory geography.
* **Giovanni Battista Piranesi** — architectural immensity and ruined engineering.
* **Ian Miller** — corroded organic-mechanical density within isolated scars.
* **Kilian Eng** — localized psychedelic/retro-futurist anomalous illumination.
* **Gustave Doré** — sublimity, chiaroscuro and small figures against immense landscapes.

Influences concern craft techniques and viewer effects; do not reproduce specific identifiable artworks or print the artist registry on the board.

Tom Patterson and Bernhard Jenny jointly inform the relief portrayal. Other artist references guide particular visual decisions without requiring printed artist credits.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/terrain-references>>
!! Real Olympus sources (research only)

These URIs serve *non-depicted reference metadata*, not subject labels or commands to insert photographic prints into the board. Each resource supplies a different observational standpoint; none establishes the final geography of Elyncia's Olympos. An AI lacking URL retrieval needs the reference supplied as an attached image or a separately described observation. Preserve source credit and original license when reusing photographic material.

* **RELIEF / terrain structure:** https://en-ca.topographic-map.com/map-ll8c18/Mount-Olympus/ — interactive elevation map of the real Greek summit area; inspect connected slopes and high relief. Its displayed elevation range covers a local map area, not a definitive full-massif survey. Role: coherent ridges, saddles and descent, not an imposed Elyncian route.
* **AERIAL / geographic appearance:** https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece_Mount-Olympus_Muses-Plateau_Mytikas-2918m_Christos-Kakkalos-Refuge_Aerial-ISymeonidis-3.jpg — aerial photograph of Mytikas and the Muses Plateau by Isymeoni (2020; CC BY-SA 4.0). Role: elevated rock-mass continuity, plateau exposure, aerial scale, not an obligation to paint the photograph itself.
* **EMBODIED / ground-level appearance:** https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olympus_Mytikas_from_Skala.jpg — view from Skala toward Mytikas (2011; Wikimedia Commons, CC0 derivative). Role: human-accessible ridge perspective, immediate drops, close geological silhouette and the traveler's implied position; the photographer/hiker does not enter the frame automatically.

These three URIs remain in this research ahu **outside the Folio copyable**. A source-aware receiver can consult them alongside the art sigils; the image-facing block carries only their relevant visual consequences. Neither the Folio nor the compact Inscription asks for printed URLs or source photographs.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/canon-boundary>>
!! Elyncia bearings and withheld claims

* Shattered Heights of Olympos, Rift of Tempes and Ash Plains of Phlegra occupy the Olympian Marches.
* Marches connect Neo-Thracia and Illyria. Wider Aegean-facing relationships remain outside the geographic claims of this particular image.
* Millennia of Gigantomachy electromagnetic, magitech and sorcerous contamination precede roughly five centuries of maelstrom enclosure.
* Following the maelstrom enclosure, the reopened corridor admits hazardous passage and pockets of survival.
* Illyria's Necrospire impact lies in the Accursed Mountains, **not** on Olympos.
* Exact route geometry, relic identity, settlements, supernatural mechanisms and boundaries remain open. The Gorgonne Diaspora counts among the established named regional powers, but no specific settlement or encounter follows automatically.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/resources>>
!! Critical and cartographic source register

* Canon bearings: Elyncia regional and nexus worksheets, the Neo-Thracia referee worksheet (restricted register), and the region-worksheet template in the Synthetic Dream Machine repository. The source files, not this exploration, govern setting claims; do not transmit unrelated regional names into image-facing instructions.
* Tom Patterson and Bernhard Jenny, *Introducing Plan Oblique Relief*: https://cartographicperspectives.org/index.php/journal/article/view/cp57-jenny-patterson
* Getty Research Institute Art & Architecture Thesaurus (AAT): https://www.getty.edu/research/conducting_research/vocabularies/aat/
* Getty Categories for the Description of Works of Art: https://www.getty.edu/publications/categories-description-works-art/categories/ ; Materials/Techniques: https://www.getty.edu/publications/categories-description-works-art/categories/object-architecture-group/7/ ; Subject Matter: https://www.getty.edu/publications/categories-description-works-art/categories/object-architecture-group/16/
* W. J. T. Mitchell, *Picture Theory*: metapictures and pictures-about-pictures.
* Kress and van Leeuwen, *Reading Images*: represented participants vs audiences and composition.
* Michael Baxandall, *Patterns of Intention*: inferred design problems and historically situated choices.
* James Gurney, *Color and Light*: specific relationships among light, material and atmosphere.
* Harvard Project Zero: https://pz.harvard.edu/resources/what-makes-you-say
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/aftermath>>
!! Aftermath — the artwork's unresolved claims

The Folio assembles terrain, contested passage, uncertain remains, habitation and their contrasting visual treatments within one artboard. The primary painted relief proposes a landscape; route studies challenge its crossing; charcoal remnants sustain contrary identifications; a green refuge complicates apparent desolation.

A path painted across a ridge cannot certify traversability. Bone-like and mechanical outlines do not establish archaeological provenance. A refuge study implies inhabitation without establishing a specific settlement. The three Olympus sources offer observational bearings, not surveys of fictional geography.

Example Zero's geography, artists, supports, colors and narrative tensions belong to this Folio and its companion Inscription, without becoming assumptions of the general grammar.
<<~/ahu>>

